**assistant**:
> That repetition is very likely expected from the current logic, not evidence that the files are changing each time.
> 
> The key issue is this test:
> 
> ```bash
> elif git -C "$LOCAL_PATH" diff --quiet -- "$relpath"; then
> ```
> 
> `git diff` only tells you whether a **tracked** file differs from the Git index/HEAD. It does **not** treat ignored/untracked files like `logs/...` as “locally modified.” So for ignored log files:
> 
> 1. local file exists,
> 2. Git says “no tracked diff,”
> 3. script prints:
> 
> ```text
> Importing remote-modified file unchanged locally: logs/news/inputs-2025-09-01.log
> ```
> 
> every single run, even if the local and remote files are byte-identical. The echo happens before `rsync`, so it is really saying “this path passed my import condition,” not “rsync transferred changed content.”
> 
> There is also a conceptual conflict:
> 
> ```bash
> REMOTE_IMPORT_PATHS=(
>     "logs"
>     "readdocs"
> )
> ```
> 
> means “copy server changes down,” but:
> 
> ```bash
> FILE_PATHS=(
>     "logs"
>     ...
> )
> ```
> 
> means “copy local version up to server, with `--delete` for directories.”
> 
> So `logs` are being round-tripped: remote → local before commit, then local → remote during deployment. If these are truly server-generated runtime artifacts, I would not include `logs` in both places.
> 
> My recommendation:
> 
> ```bash
> REMOTE_IMPORT_PATHS=(
>     "readdocs"
> )
> ```
> 
> and either remove `logs` from `FILE_PATHS` too, or only sync a carefully chosen subset of logs that you truly want local to manage.
> 
> If you want to keep importing remote logs for archival, make that a separate one-way step and avoid printing unless content actually differs. For example, replace the noisy branch with a dry-run check:
> 
> ```bash
> elif git -C "$LOCAL_PATH" diff --quiet -- "$relpath"; then
>     if rsync -azcn "$HOST:$REMOTE_PATH/$relpath" "$local_file" | grep -q .; then
>         echo "Importing changed remote file: $relpath"
>         rsync -az "$HOST:$REMOTE_PATH/$relpath" "$local_file"
>     fi
> else
>     echo "Keeping local version of locally modified file: $relpath"
> fi
> ```
> 
> But the cleaner fix is: don’t treat `logs` as both remote-imported and local-deployed state. Server-generated logs should usually stay server-side, perhaps backed up separately.
