**assistant**:
> Yes: I’d add a third category for **bidirectional merge paths**: directories where files from either side should accumulate on both sides, with **no deletion** and ideally one `rsync` per directory.
> 
> Right now `logs` is in both:
> 
> ```bash
> REMOTE_IMPORT_PATHS=( "logs" "readdocs" )
> FILE_PATHS=( "logs" ... )
> ```
> 
> That makes it both “remote-imported” and “local-mirrored,” and the per-file import loop is slow because it likely opens an `rsync`/SSH session for every file.
> 
> I’d refactor like this:
> 
> ```bash
> REMOTE_IMPORT_PATHS=(
>     "readdocs"
> )
> 
> BIDIRECTIONAL_MERGE_PATHS=(
>     "logs"
> )
> 
> FILE_PATHS=(
>     "database"
>     "knowledge/entries.txt"
>     "memory/bayesian.txt"
> )
> ```
> 
> Then replace the per-file `logs` import with directory-level rsync:
> 
> ```bash
> echo "Merging bidirectional remote state into local copy..."
> 
> for path in "${BIDIRECTIONAL_MERGE_PATHS[@]}"; do
>     mkdir -p "$LOCAL_PATH/$path"
>     ssh "$HOST" "mkdir -p -- $(printf '%q' "$REMOTE_PATH/$path")"
> 
>     rsync -azu --itemize-changes \
>         "$HOST:$REMOTE_PATH/$path/" \
>         "$LOCAL_PATH/$path/"
> done
> ```
> 
> And after the remote backup, before remote deploy, push local-side additions/updates back:
> 
> ```bash
> CURRENT_STEP="Merging bidirectional local state to remote..."
> echo "$CURRENT_STEP"
> 
> for path in "${BIDIRECTIONAL_MERGE_PATHS[@]}"; do
>     local_item="$LOCAL_PATH/$path"
>     remote_item="$REMOTE_PATH/$path"
> 
>     mkdir -p "$local_item"
>     ssh "$HOST" "mkdir -p -- $(printf '%q' "$remote_item")"
> 
>     rsync -azu --itemize-changes \
>         "$local_item/" \
>         "$HOST:$remote_item/"
> done
> ```
> 
> Important details:
> 
> - `--delete` should **not** be used for bidirectional logs.
> - `-u` / `--update` avoids overwriting a newer receiver file with an older sender file.
> - This assumes each log file has a single real writer. If both machines might append to the exact same filename, `rsync` is not a true conflict-resolution system; use host-specific log names or something like Unison/Syncthing.
> - This should be much faster because it turns hundreds/thousands of SSH+rsync calls into one call per directory.
> 
> For log names, I’d strongly prefer including hostname as well as PID, e.g. `inputs-2025-09-01-existencia-12345.log`, because PIDs alone are not globally unique.
