**assistant**:
> Yes, but I would **not** try to merge Whoosh databases by syncing their files. Whoosh segment/toc files are Lucene-like generated index artifacts; they are not safely mergeable with `rsync` or Git.
> 
> The immediate error means Git is trying to check out tracked files under:
> 
> ```text
> database/whoosh/
> ```
> 
> but the server already has untracked runtime-generated files at the same paths. So first: make Whoosh indexes **not Git-managed**.
> 
> Add to `.gitignore`:
> 
> ```gitignore
> database/whoosh/
> ```
> 
> Then locally:
> 
> ```bash
> git -C "$LOCAL_PATH" rm -r --cached database/whoosh || true
> git -C "$LOCAL_PATH" add .gitignore
> git -C "$LOCAL_PATH" commit -m "Stop tracking generated Whoosh index"
> ```
> 
> Also stop syncing the whole `database` directory blindly if it contains generated indexes. Replace:
> 
> ```bash
> FILE_PATHS=(
>     "database"
>     ...
> )
> ```
> 
> with narrower paths, excluding Whoosh, e.g.:
> 
> ```bash
> FILE_PATHS=(
>     "database/some-canonical-memory-file.sqlite"
>     "database/logs/running.log"
>     "knowledge/entries.txt"
>     "memory/bayesian.txt"
> )
> ```
> 
> For the merge design: the robust pattern is:
> 
> 1. Treat **canonical memory records** as the source of truth.
> 2. Sync/merge those records bidirectionally.
> 3. Rebuild Whoosh from the canonical records on each machine.
> 
> If the only copy of some knowledge is currently inside Whoosh, then yes: write Python scripts, but make them operate at the **document level**, not the segment-file level:
> 
> ```text
> remote Whoosh -> export JSONL
> local Whoosh  -> export JSONL
> merge JSONL by stable document id
> rebuild local Whoosh atomically
> copy/export merged canonical records to server
> rebuild server Whoosh atomically
> ```
> 
> Ideally each indexed document has fields like:
> 
> ```text
> id
> source
> created_at
> updated_at
> content
> metadata
> ```
> 
> Then merging is straightforward: union by `id`, choose newer `updated_at` for conflicts, and log true conflicts.
> 
> Deployment-wise, I’d add something like:
> 
> ```bash
> GENERATED_CLEAN_PATHS=(
>     "database/whoosh"
> )
> ```
> 
> and on the remote before `git pull`:
> 
> ```bash
> git clean -fd -- database/whoosh
> ```
> 
> But only after you’ve backed up or exported anything valuable.
> 
> My strong recommendation: make Whoosh disposable. Preserve memories in JSONL/SQLite/logs; rebuild Whoosh as a cache. That will make deployment, rollback, and cross-server synchronization much saner.
